  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1218 AM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nToday: Rainfall Potential Over Northeast Iowa and Southwest Wisconsin\n\nThe surface boundary that was situated across the Upper Mississippi \nRiver Valley over the past day or so has shifted south of the region \nand is currently draped across central Iowa into northern Illinois. \nThrough the early morning hours, an upper level shortwave lifts \nnorthward out of Nebraska and over the surface boundary, \nredeveloping showers and storms as it does so.\n\nEarlier forecasts suggested that portions of northeast Iowa and \nsouthwest Wisconsin could see a few inches in additional rainfall, \nbut the bulk of 19.18z model solutions have trended most of the \nadditional rainfall associated with this upper wave to our south \nwith only a few suggesting the surface boundary is able to nudge \nnorthward. Certainly tend towards the southern solution however as \nstronger moisture transport and deeper moisture exists to our south \nalong the aforementioned boundary. Can't rule out some transient \nshowers and perhaps a storm given a few hundred J/kg of MUCAPE \ndepicted in some solutions bringing isolated and brief heavy rain \naround 1\" (20-30%) through Sunday morning, mainly for those along \nand south of Highway 18 closer to the surface boundary, though \nportions of southwest Wisconsin south of I-90 may also see these \namounts as weak 925hPa frontogenesis sits across this region. That \nsaid, but additional flooding impacts should largely remain south of \nour area. Outside of these showers/storms, much of the area should \nbe dry throughout the day with perhaps some scattering clouds \nthroughout the afternoon.\n\nMonday: Potential for Showers Returns\n\nMonday into Tuesday brings additional potential for rainfall as the \nupper level closed low currently over the northern Rockies shifts \neastward into the Upper Midwest. Currently thinking areas along and \nsouth of I-90 will be favored to see shower activity during this \nperiod as the current trajectory of the upper low and the placement \nof a surface boundary suggests the strongest QG forcing should \nreside across these areas. \n\nRainfall amounts should be on the lower side compared to the last \nday or so with the 20.01z NBM suggesting amounts are favored to be \nunder 1 inch with only a 10-20% chance to exceed this amount. That \nsaid, some flooding potential does exist, mainly across southeast \nMinnesota and northeast Iowa, if amounts exceed 1 inch given the \nsaturated soils, but should largely be mitigated by fairly marginal \nparameters for heavy rainfall.\n\nTuesday - Friday: Cooler and Drier\n\nCooler and more fall-like temperatures are in store for the Upper \nMississippi River Valley through much of the new week as high \npressure and lower heights become situated over the Great Lakes by \nTuesday with highs in the 60s to low 70s and lows in the 40s. A \ndrier airmass also sinks southward into the Upper Midwest during \nthis period, limiting any additional rainfall potential as we head \ninto next weekend.",
